/**
\page org_mitk_views_gibbstracking Global Gibbs Tractography

This view provides the user interface for the global Gibbs tractography algorithm, a global fiber tracking algorithm, originally proposed by Reisert et.al. [1].

The corresponding command line application is named "MitkGlobalTractography".

\tableofcontents

\section QmitkGibbsTrackingUserManualInputData Input Data

Mandatory Input:
\li One ODF or tensor image selected in the datamanager

Optional Input:
\li Mask Image: Any 3D scalar image, e.g. a white matter probability mask. Particles are only placed in voxels with a value above 0.5; inside the mask the value weights the probability to generate fiber segments in the respective voxel. A segmentation can be selected too; it counts as 1 inside its labels (also hidden ones) and 0 elsewhere. Without a mask the whole image is used. A mask without any voxel above 0.5 stops the run with a message.

\section QmitkGibbsTrackingUserManualParameters Parameters

\li Number of iterations: More iterations causes the algorithm to be more stable but also to take longer to finish the tracking. Recommended: minimum 10^8 iterations for full brain tractography.
\li Particle length/width/weight controlling the contribution of each particle to the model M
\li Start and end temperature controlling how fast the process reaches a stable state. (usually no change needed)
\li Weighting between the internal (affinity of the model to long and straigt fibers) and external energy (affinity of the model towards the data). (usually no change needed).
\li Minimum fiber length constraint (in mm). Shorter fibers are discarded after the tracking.
\li Curvature threshold (in degree): maximum angle between two connected particles. Values above 90 degree act as 90 degree.
\li Random seed: "auto" draws a new seed for every run; any other value reproduces the run exactly.
\li Parallel sampler: updates blocks of the particle grid on several threads at the same time, which is much faster on whole-brain data. With a fixed random seed it gives the same tractogram at any number of threads, but not the same tractogram as the default (serial) sampler. Since 10 to 20% of its proposals are rejected at block borders, its fibers grow more slowly per iteration; give it more iterations. In a whole-brain test (HCP data, 1.25 mm, brain mask, 24 threads on 12 cores) it ran the same number of iterations 20 times as fast as the serial sampler, and with twice the iterations it gave longer fibers than the serial sampler in a seventh of the time.
\li Threads: the number of threads of the parallel sampler; "all cores" uses as many as OpenMP does (all cores, unless OMP_NUM_THREADS says otherwise).

The command line application offers the parallel sampler as --parallel and --threads. Like the random seed, neither is saved in the parameter file.

The automatic selection of parameters for the particle length/width and weight are determined directly from the input image using information about the image spacing and GFA. After a run, the values used are shown next to "auto"; the parameters stay automatic for the next run. When the particle weight cannot be estimated (e.g. only isotropic ODFs inside the mask), the run stops with a message; set the weight by hand then.

"Save Parameters" and "Load Parameters" write and read the parameters as .gtp file (curvature threshold in degree), which the command line application reads too. A file that is not a complete parameter file is reported and leaves the view unchanged; loaded values are rounded to the steps of the sliders.

\li Output File: the tractogram is added to the data storage and, if an output file is selected, also saved to that file when the run ends. A run that gives no fibers is reported with a message.

\section QmitkGibbsTrackingUserManualTrackingSurveillance Surveillance of the tracking process

Once started, the tracking can be monitored via the textual output that informs about the tracking progress and several stats of the current state of the algorithm.
The statistics (progress, particles, connections, acceptance rate, elapsed time) are updated every second. If enabled, the intermediate tracking results are displayed in the renderwindows each second as well. This live visualization should usually be disabled for performance reasons. It can be turned on and off during the tracking process via the according checkbox. The button next to this checkbox shows the current state once. The intermediate results do not change the final result. "Stop" ends the run also during the automatic estimation of the particle weight.

\section QmitkGibbsTrackingUserManualReferences References

[1] Reisert, M., Mader, I., Anastasopoulos, C., Weigel, M., Schnell, S., Kiselev, V.: Global fiber reconstruction becomes practical. Neuroimage 54 (2011) 955-962


*/
